item.text:
Reused evidence: none. The previously accepted 1B items are not materially useful for 2B, so I did not reuse them.

New evidence:
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"MFA weekly briefing on Ukraine, 3 March 2022","publisher":"Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Kingdom of Thailand","publication_date":"2022-03-04","url":"https://www.mfa.go.th/en/content/weeklypressbriefing03032565","claim":"Thailand said it was following the Ukraine crisis with deep concern, supported peaceful settlement through dialogue, and voted in favor of the 2 March 2022 UN resolution because of the UN Charter, international law, sovereignty, and territorial integrity.","locator":"lines 172-196","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary Thai MFA record of Thailand's policy explanation and UN vote position in March 2022.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 ruler-period and directly relevant to Thailand's international-security posture on Ukraine.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Thai foreign ministry under Prayut's government; not a private PM statement but an official government position.","contrary_evidence":["Subsequent Thai abstentions show the line was not uniformly interventionist."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.6","2B.7","2B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"UN General Assembly adopts resolution on Ukraine, 2 March 2022","publisher":"United Nations","publication_date":"2022-03-02","url":"https://press.un.org/en/2022/ga12407.doc.htm","claim":"The General Assembly demanded that Russia immediately cease its unlawful use of force against Ukraine, withdraw all military forces, and pursue a peaceful resolution through political dialogue, negotiations, mediation, and other peaceful means while protecting civilians.","locator":"search result snippet","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary UN record of the resolution and its peace/humanitarian language.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly in 2022 and provides the legal-diplomatic frame for Thailand's supportive vote and later abstentions.","ruler_attribution":"This is UN context rather than Prayut-specific conduct, but it anchors the decision Thailand was responding to.","contrary_evidence":["The press record is not Thailand-specific and does not by itself establish Thai attribution."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.4","2B.7"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"MFA meeting with UNSG Special Envoy on Myanmar","publisher":"Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Kingdom of Thailand","publication_date":"2022-01-15","url":"https://mfa.go.th/en/content/se141165?cate=5d5bcb4e15e39c306000683e&menu=5d5bd3da15e39c306002aafa","claim":"Thailand told the UN Secretary-General's Special Envoy on Myanmar that violence near the Thai-Myanmar border was causing civilians to flee into Thailand and that Thailand was ready to cooperate with UN and ASEAN mechanisms to find peaceful solutions and deliver humanitarian assistance.","locator":"search result snippet","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary Thai MFA statement with clear date, named interlocutor, and peace/humanitarian commitments.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and directly relevant to Thailand's handling of Myanmar spillover.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Thai foreign policy apparatus under Prayut's government; Don Pramudwinai acted as deputy prime minister and foreign minister.","contrary_evidence":["This is a diplomatic posture statement, not proof of successful conflict resolution."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thailand scrambles fighters after Myanmar jet airspace breach","publisher":"Reuters via MarketScreener","publication_date":"2022-01-07","url":"https://www.marketscreener.com/amp/news/latest/Thailand-scrambles-fighters-after-Myanmar-jet-airspace-breach--40866450/","claim":"After Thai fighters were scrambled in response to a Myanmar combat aircraft briefly entering Thai airspace, Prayut Chan-o-cha said the incident was 'not a big deal' and noted that a Myanmar defense attache had apologized.","locator":"search result snippet","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters reporting with a direct Prayut quote and clear border-security context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and relevant to a cross-border security episode involving a neighboring conflict.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Prayut's public handling of the border incident, though the underlying military response was carried out by Thai air force and defense officials.","contrary_evidence":["Thailand still deployed fighters, so the episode was not purely verbal de-escalation."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.3","2B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Statement by H.E. Mr. Don Pramudwinai at the General Debate of the 77th Session of the United Nations General Assembly","publisher":"Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Kingdom of Thailand","publication_date":"2022-09-24","url":"https://www.mfa.go.th/en/content/dpmunga240965-2","claim":"Thailand said that, as Myanmar's immediate neighbor, it had a vital interest in a quick return to peace and stability there, called on all parties to de-escalate, end violence, and engage in meaningful talks, and said it would continue to play an active and constructive role with ASEAN and the UN special envoy.","locator":"lines 212-214","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary Thai government speech at the UN with explicit statements on Myanmar, humanitarian assistance, and ASEAN process.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and directly relevant to Thailand's regional peace posture.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Don Pramudwinai as Thailand's deputy PM/foreign minister under Prayut; an official cabinet position rather than a personal PM statement.","contrary_evidence":["The speech is aspirational and does not itself prove Myanmar policy success."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thai MFA summary of informal consultation on Myanmar, 23 Dec 2022","publisher":"Ministry of Foreign Affairs, Kingdom of Thailand","publication_date":"2022-12-23","url":"https://www.mfa.go.th/th/content/presscon23122022?menu=5d5bd3c915e39c306002a909&page=5d5bd3c915e39c306002a907","claim":"Thailand hosted an informal consultation on Myanmar with Cambodia, Laos, Myanmar, and Vietnam, describing it as open, constructive, and pragmatic and focused especially on humanitarian assistance and ways to support the five-point consensus.","locator":"section 'การหารืออย่างไม่เป็นทางการเกี่ยวกับสถานการณ์ในเมียนมา (๒๒ ธ.ค. ๒๕๖๕)'","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary Thai-language MFA summary of a dated 2022 meeting with precise participants and purpose.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and directly relevant to Thailand's handling of the Myanmar crisis.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Thai foreign ministry under Prayut's government; Don Pramudwinai hosted the meeting.","contrary_evidence":["Critics could see the meeting as breaching ASEAN norms, but the source itself frames it as pragmatic peace facilitation."],"lenses":["2B.7","2B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Myanmar shadow govt raises alarm after junta meets ASEAN members","publisher":"Reuters via Euronews","publication_date":"2022-12-24","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/12/24/thailand-myanmar","claim":"Thailand hosted junta ministers and other Southeast Asian officials for informal Myanmar talks aimed at a diplomatic path toward normalcy and the five-point peace process, but the meeting drew criticism from ASEAN actors who said it risked breaching the bloc's exclusion of junta representatives.","locator":"search result snippet","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters reporting with both the host government's framing and the main contrary criticism.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and directly relevant to Thailand's regional conflict mediation posture.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Thailand's foreign ministry and government hosting role, not to Prayut personally ordering a military operation.","contrary_evidence":["Several ASEAN members skipped the meeting and critics said it was not helpful and could breach ASEAN policy on junta representation."],"lenses":["2B.5","2B.7","2B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"General Assembly Adopts Text to Suspend Russian Federation from Human Rights Council, Continuing Emergency Special Session on Humanitarian Crisis in Ukraine","publisher":"United Nations","publication_date":"2022-04-07","url":"https://press.un.org/en/2022/ga12414.doc.htm","claim":"Thailand abstained on the HRC suspension resolution because it said suspending any UN member should not be taken lightly, supported an independent commission of inquiry, expressed concern over the humanitarian crisis, and renewed the call for political dialogue to end the dispute.","locator":"search result snippet","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary UN meeting record capturing the Thai delegation's explanation of vote.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and directly relevant to Thailand's diplomatic posture on the Ukraine war.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Thailand's UN delegation under Prayut's government; this is state diplomacy, not a personal PM statement.","contrary_evidence":["Abstention is weaker than direct support for condemnation and can be read as hedging rather than clear alignment."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.6","2B.7","2B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thai PBS report on Thailand abstaining on Ukraine annexation resolution","publisher":"Thai PBS","publication_date":"2022-10-13","url":"https://www.thaipbs.or.th/news/content/320431/","claim":"Thailand abstained on the UN resolution condemning Russia's annexation of Ukrainian territory while the Thai foreign ministry said Thailand still upheld the UN Charter, sovereignty, territorial integrity, and opposition to the use of force.","locator":"opening summary and numbered explanation in article body","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Thai-language independent reporting of the government's stated rationale, contemporaneous with the vote.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and directly relevant to Thailand's Ukraine policy stance.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Thai foreign ministry under Prayut's government; not an individual military order.","contrary_evidence":["The abstention reduced the force of Thailand's public opposition to annexation compared with a yes vote."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.3","2B.6","2B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Thailand abstains in UN vote against Russia over Ukraine war","publisher":"Nation Thailand","publication_date":"2022-11-16","url":"https://www.nationthailand.com/world/russia-central-asia/40022114","claim":"Thailand abstained when the UN General Assembly called on Russia to bear legal consequences and make reparations for injuries and damage caused by the war in Ukraine, even as several ASEAN states voted for the resolution.","locator":"lines 23-29","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous English-language report with the vote result and Thailand's position clearly stated.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and directly relevant to Thailand's stance on Russian aggression and accountability.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Thailand's government and UN delegation under Prayut, not to direct military command.","contrary_evidence":["Thailand's abstention once again avoided a stronger anti-aggression vote despite support from some ASEAN members."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.3","2B.6","2B.9","2B.10"]}
- SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"General Assembly Adopts Text Recommending Creation of Register to Document Damages Caused by Russian Federation Aggression against Ukraine","publisher":"United Nations","publication_date":"2022-11-14","url":"https://press.un.org/en/2022/ga12470.doc.htm","claim":"Thailand abstained on a resolution creating a register of damage from the war in Ukraine; the UN text said Russia must be held to account for violations of international law and the need for a reparations mechanism.","locator":"search result snippet","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary UN meeting record with the vote and accountability framing; directly contemporaneous.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within 2022 and relevant to Thailand's accountability posture on the Ukraine war.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Thailand's government and UN delegation under Prayut's cabinet.","contrary_evidence":["Thailand's abstention again fell short of a clear pro-accountability vote."],"lenses":["2B.6","2B.9","2B.10"]}

Search and rejection summary:
- I considered roughly 30 documents across Thai MFA pages, UN press releases, Reuters-syndicated reports, Thai PBS, Nation Thailand, and Reuters mirrors hosted by Euronews/MarketScreener/ThePrint.
- I retained only sources that added something materially distinct: Thailand's March 2022 pro-dialogue Ukraine vote explanation, repeated 2022 abstentions, the January and September Myanmar diplomacy record, the December Myanmar consultation, and the January border-airspace incident.
- I rejected duplicate retellings of the same vote or Myanmar meeting from multiple publishers when they did not add a new claim.
- I excluded broad Ukraine explainers, generic ASEAN background, and non-2022 recaps as weak or out-of-period.
- I also dropped blocked/failed fetches and 2023-2024 follow-up pieces that only restated the same 2022 stance.
- The result is a mixed but narrow evidence set: mostly state diplomacy, not battlefield conduct.

Remaining gaps:
- No source shows Prayut personally initiating, escalating, or directing an interstate war, proxy war, or unlawful use of force in 2022.
- No source shows civilian harm, unlawful military operations, or proxy atrocities attributable to Prayut in an international conflict, which is consistent with the local prior showing zero internationalized conflict events and fatalities.
- The strongest evidence is about diplomatic posture on Ukraine and Myanmar, not hard security command. That makes Chapter 2B a limited-exposure case unless additional ruler-specific conduct is uncovered.
- More search would only help if you want to pin down a direct PM-level order or hidden role in the Myanmar consultations or the Myanmar border incident.
